.TH auto-add-to-group 8

\" auto-man2man (used in generating subcommand lists) depends on this section
.SH NAME
.PP
auto-add-to-group - Add a user to a group

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-add-to-group user-name group-name
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
user-name   Name of the user to add to the group
group-name  Name of the group to which the user is added
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-add-to-group
command adds the user "user-name" to the group "group-name".
In other words, "group-name" is added to the list of supplementary groups
for "user-name".  The primary group for "user-name" is unaffected.

Native tools for this vary widely across operating systems, creating
hurdles for those who want to automate management of
multiple different systems.
Auto-admin(1) thus serves and
a compatibility layer, removing platform-specific code from
numerous higher-level projects.

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-add-to-group joeuser projectx
.ad
.fi

.SH FILES
.nf
.na
/etc/group
.ad
.fi

.SH SEE ALSO

auto-admin(8)

